{"episodes":{"210813":"On April 2nd's early evening, an upper level short wave disturbance crossing the Middle Mississppi Valley, and associated 1000-mb surface low pressure tracking quickly from Iowa to Wiscomsin, placed central Indiana within its warm sector.  The conditional set-up for severe weather included steep 0-3 km lapse rates, and corresponding DCAPE values of 500-800 J/kg extending along and just east of the Wabash Valley.  A resultant quasi-linear convective system (QLCS) crossed western central Indiana, producing widely scattered damaging straight line winds.  The system's overall high cloud base mitigated any tornado production except where a brief EF-0 was spawned in Parke County.","210814":"On April 3rd, severe thunderstorms swept through the central Indiana region, with Warren County specifically targeted by an isolated stronger storm that tracked across the county, producing 1 inch hail near the Tippecanoe County border. The hail was part of a broader severe system that also triggered tornado warnings in adjacent Benton County.","211371":"Scattered thunderstorms moved across portions of northern Illinois during the evening of April 2nd producing wind damage and three tornadoes.","211372":"Scattered thunderstorms moved across southern Lake Michigan during the evening of April 2nd.","211763":"A high-amplitude (closed) upper storm system moved eastward across the Rockies on the 3rd and fostered a severe convective episode across portions of Oklahoma and western-north Texas. Initial thunderstorm development during the afternoon focused along a cold front from southwest into north-central Oklahoma, with a mix of multicell and supercell storm modes producing hail. Later in the evening, an intense complex of thunderstorms moved from west-central into east-central Oklahoma, including across the Oklahoma City Metropolitan area. This cluster produced swaths of damaging winds and several tornadoes. By the late evening, increasing frontal thunderstorms moved towards the Red River, producing intermittent wind gusts/damage and flooding.","212006":"During the afternoon and evening of April 2nd, 2026,  and early spring supercell thunderstorm event spread across eastern Iowa and northwest Illinois. This brought several swaths of damaging wind, large hail, and several tornadoes to both Iowa and Illinois.","212144":"A broken line of thunderstorms developed over Illinois in the afternoon and tracked into northern Indiana. Wind gusts of 55 mph to 65 mph were recorded resulting in at least one instance of tree and property damage.","212327":"Isolated severe storms over southwest Georgia resulted in wind damage and large hail.","212430":"Strong to severe thunderstorms occurred along a warm front in southern WI and produced damaging winds across southeastern WI as it pushed out over Lake Michigan.","212450":"At the surface, a low wobbled eastward from southern QC to northern ME. This allowed the trailing cold front to sweep generally southeastward across northern OH and northwest PA toward southern OH and southwestern PA. Along and ahead of the front, clusters of multicell thunderstorms persisted generally eastward across interior northern OH and generated isolated straight-line convective wind damage and marginally-severe hail in Marion, Morrow, and Holmes Counties. These thunderstorms occurred in a relatively-warm and moist sector comprised of weak to moderate MUCAPE and moderate effective bulk shear.","212464":"Low pressure and a warm front brought a round of severe thunderstorms to southern WI during the evening. Large hail and damaging straight-line winds occurred.","212486":"Strong to severe thunderstorms traversed southeast Nebraska during the afternoon hours of April 3rd, 2026. Ahead of the convective activity, a low pressure system lifted northeast over eastern Nebraska and western Iowa. An associated warm front lifted into east-central Nebraska and west-central Iowa, while a cold front trailed behind it. Within the warm sector, temperatures warmed into the 60s and 70s through the day. Thunderstorms developed along the front during the afternoon. As these storms moved through southeast Nebraska, the public reported quarter-sized hail (estimated at one inch in diameter) 3 miles northeast of Shubert, NE at 2:02 PM CST.","212655":"A cluster of thunderstorms developed across south-central Kansas during the afternoon hours. These thunderstorms produce large hail near Andover.","212734":"Enter narrative here.","212777":"A wet pattern developed during the first days of the month as above normal moisture, a nearby upper-level trough and a weak  trade wind cap supported shower and thunderstorm development. This included frequent passing showers across windward sectors, followed by enhanced afternoon convection. Flooding and Landslide reports where received during this period, April 1st to 5th.","213043":"A line of thunderstorms moved across south central and southeast Kansas during the afternoon and evening hours of April 3rd. A few reports of hail were received.","213194":"A warm and moist airmass was in place across southeast Louisiana on April 2nd, with scattered to numerous thunderstorms developing during the afternoon and evening hours. Storms became oriented north to south and repeatedly redeveloped while slowly shifting east across Tangipahoa Parish. This allowed heavy rainfall to fall over some of the same areas for an extended period. A stronger storm also produced localized wind damage near Tickfaw."},"events":[{"begin":"2026-04-03T00:03Z","county":"Putnam","damageCrops":"0.00K","damageProperty":"0.00K","deaths":0,"end":"2026-04-03T00:03Z","endLat":41.26,"endLon":-89.33,"episode":212006,"id":1325171,"injuries":0,"lat":41.26,"location":"1.25 ENE Hennepin","lon":-89.33,"magnitude":53.0,"magnitudeType":"MG","narrative":"Public measured wind equipement.","source":"Public","state":"IL","type":"Thunderstorm Wind","wfo":"DVN"},{"begin":"2026-04-03T00:03Z","county":"Bureau","damageCrops":"0